+\section{Reserved Follow-up: Gradients, Groups and Robust Reweighting}
+\label{sec:revision-research-closure}
+
+The preceding studies motivated three separate follow-ups. Each retains its
+own representation and source history: native-gradient aggregation, selection
+of retrospective investigation groups, and robust aggregation after admission.
+All new predictive rates in this section use the retained Data24 validation
+fold. The test results in earlier sections are not recomputed or pooled with
+them. New attacker assignments are reserved experiment cases on the same two
+data partitions, rather than independently collected datasets.
+
+\subsection{Closing the Native-gradient Comparison}
+\label{subsec:revision-native-gradient-closure}
+
+The earlier interrupted experiment in
+Section~\ref{subsec:revision-fedgradtrace} is retained as a development result.
+The follow-up specifies one class-weighted full-snapshot cross-entropy
+gradient per honest client at the current global base, learning rate 0.001
+and a history of at most three gradients. It compares gradient FedAvg, the
+literal displayed Eq.~(2), the literal displayed Eq.~(3), and a separately
+named normalized sample-count--trajectory-norm adaptation. The latter is an
+ablation, not an undocumented correction of the publication. This is a study
+of server-side aggregation readings, not a reproduction of the complete
+FedXLog transformer and explanation system.
+
+Data-poisoned gradients alone either fail to produce a selective backdoor or
+achieve high ASR through broad predictive collapse in development. The final
+attack therefore explicitly forges malicious gradient submissions. For an
+attacker, an endpoint trained by ten Adam epochs on component-poisoned data
+defines $g^{\mathrm{forge}}=(B-U^{\mathrm{poison}})/0.001$. The submitted
+vector interpolates between this vector and the true clean gradient. Honest
+clients continue to submit actual derivatives. Half of each attacker's rows
+receive its assigned component, using features 13, 17 and 24 with value 12
+and target class benign. Attack optimization has privileged validation access:
+ten declared interpolation factors are searched to maximize the worst full-
+or component-trigger ASR, subject to at most five percentage points of
+ordinary macro-F1 loss from the same-round clean gradient-FedAvg reference.
+An infeasible search records a zero-factor fallback; it is not a successful
+attack.
+
+The reserved study has six new attacker assignments across IID and non-IID
+partitions. Clean training is deterministic here, so each cohort/method has
+one clean history rather than three artificial seed replications. Of 32
+planned histories, 25 complete fifteen rounds and seven terminate with
+reproduced numerical failures, giving 390 valid round outputs. The literal
+Eq.~(2) attacked IID histories fail after one valid round; its other retained
+failures and one clean failure are reported with their valid prefixes. No
+later checkpoint is fabricated. All three IID gradient-FedAvg controls and
+the Eq.~(3) and normalized-adaptation attacked histories reach 100\% full-
+trigger ASR with retained ordinary utility. The non-IID gradient-FedAvg
+controls peak at zero, zero and approximately 15\%; they do not satisfy the
+declared 20-percentage-point effective-control condition and cannot establish
+a mitigation advantage for that partition.
+
+A second comparison removes the incoming-state difference between methods.
+It applies seven server rules to 120 common contexts: thirty clean and ninety
+attacked gradient-FedAvg states. FLAME, WeiDetect and the original gated
+composite receive the one-step parameter representation $B-0.001g$ derived
+from exactly those submitted vectors. All 840 conditional outputs exist.
+Their peaks and exclusion counts are given in
+Table~\ref{tab:research-shared-gradient}. These are current-round comparisons,
+not seven self-driven future trajectories or attacks optimized separately
+against every defender. In particular, literal Eq.~(2) can return a finite
+output on a stable FedAvg input while failing under its own recurrence.
+
+\input{content/chapters/research_shared_table}
+
+Excluding a manipulated current export does not repair the incoming poisoned
+base. FLAME excludes all 168 currently manipulated exports in this conditional
+comparison, and the composite excludes 162, yet both retain a 100\% peak on
+some inherited IID states. FLAME also excludes 557 of the 1,632 currently
+unmodified exports, compared with one for the composite. These counts do not
+predict the outcome of a fresh actively defended history. The native norm-
+weighted rules can also amplify a forged vector into broad utility collapse:
+their low attacked-context F1 must be read alongside high ASR, rather than
+interpreting every such output as a selective backdoor.
+
+The native norm-weighting rules are not malicious-client classifiers; their
+detection rate is undefined. Exclusion counts for the other rules refer to
+currently manipulated exports, not permanent adversarial ownership. A
+subsequent unmodified submission from a formerly attacking client belongs
+in the unmodified denominator. WeiDetect's initial-model-correct auxiliary
+subset uses confidence at least 0.90 and keeps twelve clients. Degenerate
+all-equal scores invoke the declared neutral-CDF/seeded-tie extension; those
+cases are not successful Weibull fits. The composite uses clean gradient
+references and a software admissibility assumption, not fresh TPM evidence.
+These information budgets remain different and explicit.
+
+\subsection{Reserved Group Selection and Interventions}
+\label{subsec:revision-reserved-groups}
+
+The additional selection rule V3 scores a triple by the mean of its two
+strongest pair edges, equivalent to the maximum-spanning-tree mean for three
+vertices. It can retain a triple when one edge is weak, but does not claim
+that the missing edge is strong. Pair and triple families have separate
+clean calibration maxima; triple candidates are ranked before pairs.
+Fitting and calibration use the earlier clean histories. Twelve new source
+histories, six clean and six distributed-component attacks, are selected
+from observables before their evaluation labels are read. This is a recorded
+data-flow separation, not hardware isolation from the experiment controller.
+
+On the six attacked histories, V2 selects 8 true members, 3 false members and
+misses 10 of the 18 attacker occurrences. V3 selects 9 true members and 6
+false members, missing 9. Both raise a candidate in one of the six new clean
+histories. V3's broader candidate pool also increases false members from
+16 to 48. The alternative is therefore not promoted as uniformly superior
+and does not authorize automatic client exclusion. A successful IID attack
+with 60.29\% source ASR receives an empty selection from both rules.
+
+Method-selected sets are evaluated through 106 direct outputs and 23
+continuation branches. Individual removals, joint removals, benign controls
+and an exact no-removal continuation are kept distinct. In one IID case,
+the three-member set contains two attackers and one benign client. Joint
+removal changes round-10 full-trigger ASR from 57.66\% to 0.50\%, compared
+with 41.62--61.75\% after individual removals and 73.75--75.94\% after
+same-size benign-control removals. Retraining rounds 11--15 from the corrected
+base reaches zero measured ASR. In another IID case, V2's two true members
+reduce ASR from 61.64\% to 2.30\%, with zero after continuation; V3 adds a
+false third member. Conversely, a non-IID source with 99.44\% ASR remains at
+98.21\% after V2 joint removal and 16.55\% after continuation. V3 gives
+93.66\% and 14.64\%, respectively. These differences measure the effects of
+declared interventions, not proof of collusive intent or complete historical
+decontamination.
+
