+\subsection{Reserved Robust-bounded Aggregation}
+\label{subsec:revision-reserved-robust}
+
+The optional operator in
+Section~\ref{subsec:formal-robust-reweighting} changes the combination of
+already admitted, clipped updates. It retains the existing observable
+envelope, gates, quorum and clean-derived tensor limits. Development on the
+disclosed earlier counterexample reduces peak ASR from 99.50\% to 55.52\%
+and final ASR from 46.89\% to 1.46\%. Its finite rule is then frozen before
+six new attacker assignments, with paired clean and adaptive histories for
+the old bounded mean and the new robust operator.
+
+\input{content/chapters/research_robust_table}
+
+All twelve robust histories complete fifteen rounds. One attacked bounded
+control has no valid fifteenth output; its peak is measured on the retained
+fourteen-round prefix, not an invented endpoint. The maximum paired clean
+macro-F1 loss is 0.90 percentage points, within the declared two-point
+guardrail. Only two bounded controls meet the attack-effectiveness condition.
+Their full-trigger peaks change from 95.74\% to 75.72\%, and from 83.57\%
+to zero. The residual 75.72\% peak precludes a general prevention claim even
+though all six attacked robust endpoints have zero measured full- and
+component-trigger ASR.
+
+The unchanged admission rule quarantines none of the 180 manipulated exports
+in these robust histories; no currently unmodified exports are quarantined.
+The improvement is numerical reweighting, not improved malicious-client
+identification. Final attacked models remain different from their matched
+clean models. Zero ASR does not establish clean-state recovery. The fixed-
+final-coefficient removal certificates establish their parameter bound only;
+they do not bound a recomputed robust operator, a decision margin or future
+training. Figure~\ref{fig:research-robust-asr} shows why the peak and final
+measurements must both remain visible.
